  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 124 AM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nGoing into Monday, we will continue to see influence from the \nbuilding high pressure over the Great Lakes Region. A greater push \nof dry air will work into the area, allowing for the potential for \nmore breaks in the clouds. This will be a welcome sight for those \nthat have been socked in clouds and light precipitation this \nweekend. Overall, we are expecting to remain dry through Monday,\nwith temperatures warming to near 70 throughout. \n\nThis period of dryness will come to an end mid-week as a larger \nsystem builds in sometime around Tuesday/Wednesday. A longer wave \ntrough, with models showing a strong tropical connection to the \nsystems from the eastern Pacific and the Gulf, should translate \nthrough the area, although the exact onset/ending time of associated \nprecipitation remains uncertain. This is pretty clearly shown from \nthe LREF ensemble cluster analysis, which shows various depictions \nof the longer wave trough aloft. Some show a large progressive \ntrough sweeping across the central CONUS, while some show a slower \ncut-off low with multiple rounds of rain mid to late week. However, \nthere remains a strong signal for abundant moisture with this system \ngiven the tropical connection, with Pwat values progged around 1.6 \nto 2 inches, which is quite high for late September. In fact, these \nvalues would be in excess of the 99th percentile for Pwats per the \nNAEFS climatological percentiles. NBM exceedance probabilities for \none inch or more of rain for Tuesday night and Wednesday are around \n40-60%, which is actually a pretty decent signal given we're talking \nseveral days out. Despite the uncertainty on the exact duration and \nmagnitude of rainfall amounts, this signal appears supportive of \nheavy rainfall and perhaps a flash flooding threat, given wet \nantecedent conditions. So, this mid-week system bears watching. With \nthis, WPC has much of our area in a Day 5 Slight Risk (level 2 of 4) \nin their latest excessive rainfall outlooks.",
